Windows Registry Editor Version 5.00

; ===========================================================================
; Enable H.264/AVC 444 graphics mode for Remote Desktop
;
; RUN THIS ON THE MACHINE YOU CONNECT *TO* (the RDP host), NOT on the client.
; These are server-side encoder settings. Putting them on your Windows 11
; client does nothing unless that client is also an RDP target.
;
; Supported on: Windows 10 / Windows Server 2016 and later.
;   -> Server 2016, 2019, 2022, 2025 and Windows 10 / 11 all qualify.
;   -> Older (Server 2012 R2, Windows 8.1) support only AVC 4:2:0, not 444.
;
; Requires admin. Restart the host (or the Remote Desktop Services service)
; afterwards.
;
; Revert with disable-h264-avc444.reg in this folder.
; ===========================================================================

[HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows NT\Terminal Services]

; Prioritize H.264/AVC 444 Graphics mode for Remote Desktop connections.
; Used when both client and server support AVC 444 (non-vGPU scenarios).
; 4:4:4 chroma keeps text sharp; plain AVC 4:2:0 blurs it.
"AVC444ModePreferred"=dword:00000001

; Configure H.264/AVC hardware encoding for Remote Desktop connections.
; Needs a GPU exposing an H.264 encoder through Media Foundation. Without one
; the host falls back to software encoding, which costs CPU per session -
; check CPU use after enabling if the host has no capable GPU.
; Each remote monitor consumes one hardware encoder.
"AVCHardwareEncodePreferred"=dword:00000001
